// ═══ THE PROBLEM — data leaking between tasks ═══
ThreadLocal<String> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

// Task 1: sets ThreadLocal for "Rajesh"
pool.submit(() -> {
    currentUser.set("Rajesh");
    processTransaction();
    // FORGOT to remove()!
});

// Task 2: runs on the SAME worker thread
pool.submit(() -> {
    String user = currentUser.get();
    // user = "Rajesh"! Task-2 never set it!
    // Processing Priya's request with RAJESH'S context → SECURITY BUG!
});

// ═══ THE FIX — always remove() in finally ═══
pool.submit(() -> {
    try {
        currentUser.set("Rajesh");
        processTransaction();
    } finally {
        currentUser.remove();  // ALWAYS! Even if exception thrown!
    }
});

// ═══ MEMORY LEAK — ThreadLocal values never GC'd in pool ═══
// ThreadLocal is stored in Thread.threadLocals (a ThreadLocalMap)
// Key: WeakReference<ThreadLocal>
// Value: STRONG reference to the stored object!
// In a pool: thread lives FOREVER → ThreadLocalMap entries NEVER cleaned
// Even if the ThreadLocal variable goes out of scope:
//   Key (weak ref) → GC'd
//   Value (strong ref) → STILL in the map! → memory leak!
// 200 threads × 1000 tasks × 1MB per value = 200GB leaked!

// ═══ InheritableThreadLocal — same problem, worse! ═══
// InheritableThreadLocal copies parent thread's values to child threads.
// In a pool: parent sets value → pool thread INHERITS it → value persists forever!
// FIX: same — always remove() in finally.

// ═══ SPRING: RequestContextHolder uses ThreadLocal ═══
// Spring stores the current HttpServletRequest in a ThreadLocal.
// If async processing runs on a pool thread:
//   Thread may still hold the PREVIOUS request's context!
//   FIX: Spring cleans up via DispatcherServlet.processRequest() → finally block.
//   But custom ThreadLocals: YOUR responsibility to clean up!
